  4. [1mChange detection[0m — knowing a task changed, and what changed
  5. [1mDelivery[0m — the part you actually asked for

  Each of 1–4 is a prerequisite, and each has real design questions of its own. I'd rather decompose this into
  sub-projects and spec the first one properly than write a notifications spec resting on four invented assumptions.

  Before I propose that decomposition, one fork decides most of it:

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this app has a server, or is a single-user page in one browser.

  - [1mMulti-user with a server[0m — "users" is real, notifications can reach someone who isn't looking at the page (email,
    push), and changes from one person reach another. Costs: accounts, auth, a backend, a datastore, deployment. This is
    the expensive-to-reverse choice; retrofitting it later means rewriting storage and most of the task model.
  - [1mSingle-user, browser-only[0m — tasks in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, notifications are in-app toasts or the browser Notification API.
    Cheap, works today, no infrastructure. But "users" collapses to one person, and "notified when [3mthey[0m change
    something" is mostly self-notification — due-date and reminder alerts are the honest use case. Rules out
    cross-person notifications until you add a server.
  - [1mSingle-user now, server-shaped later[0m — build browser-only but keep the storage layer behind an interface so a
    backend can slot in. Middle cost, and the seam is cheap to keep if you draw it at the start, expensive to add later.

  [1mMy recommendation: tell me which of these you actually want before I design anything[0m — but if the app is real and
  meant for a team, take the server. If this is your own task list, browser-only is right and the notification story
  changes from "someone changed your task" to "this task needs you."
